Basically, UV light "kills" various bacteria, viruses, or algae cells. It can kill any type of bacteria, even nitrifying bacteria which is very useful for the aquarium cleaning process.
Having an aquarium UV lamp can be quite helpful. These are the different advantages it has:
It can also remove cloudiness from water caused by algae and bacteria.
You will get excellent results with less energy consumption.
It will not affect the power of your aquarium filter.
Also, it will not harm the beneficial bacteria colonies on the filter.
UV lamps for aquariums are usually insulated, so only the water will be affected by their radiation. It is important to remember that we should not look directly at the light because it can affect us.
Professionals, hobbyists, and manufacturers disagree on how long aquarium UV lamps should work. Although some people think that letting it run for 3 to 4 hours a day is enough, others think that it needs to run all day without any problems and it will not affect the balance of the aquarium.
In the case of UV bulbs, they lose effectiveness as they are used, so it is recommended to replace them before the end of their useful life, even if they are not burned out.
There are some amateurs who claim that the lamp can sterilize the compost that is used for plants. Many fertilizers are made up of chelates and iron and can be sterilized by UV radiation.
