When we have fish in the aquarium, if we mix specimens of the same male and female species, sooner or later they will end up mating. Contrary to what happens with the fish that inhabit their natural ecosystem, both mating and reproduction depend on the species of fish and how you have the aquarium arranged.
Viviparous fish: there are some viviparous fish that have internal fertilization similar to that of mammals. In this case, the males fertilize the female inside.
The carp species mate in the same way and can lay thousands of eggs. Although the only difference is that they are capable of eating the eggs and even the young once they hatch.
For this type of mating of fish in the aquarium to occur it is necessary that the fish tank has decorative elements that serve as rocks or some place of protection. Keep in mind that fish must feel protected and sheltered for mating.
Another way of mating is oral incubation, which consists of the female laying her eggs at the bottom of the aquarium. The male comes next and fertilizes the eggs, after which the female collects the eggs and incubates them in her mouth until they hatch.
