Sounding exactly the same, these pairs of words can confuse us in a conversation or lead us to use one instead of the other in a written text. For these reasons, it is important to know their respective meanings, in order to learn to differentiate them well and not to play tricks on us when speaking or writing in English.
Complement / Compliment: «Complement» is a noun or a verb whose translation into Spanish is complement or complement. For example: "This hat is a perfect complement to my outfit" ("This hat is a perfect complement to my outfit").
Main / Principle: «Principal» is used to refer to someone who occupies the highest position («the school principal» / «the principal of the school») or to express that someone or something has the first place in estimation or importance with respect to others ( "That was the main reason" / "That was the main reason"). "Principle" in Spanish is "principle."
