For this reason, when we know who were these men, women and children who lived long before Christopher Columbus arrived in America, we can say without any doubt that they were people who were very advanced.
1 Where are the Mayans located?
To understand who the Mayans were, we must first know where they are located. Basically this town that gave the world great beliefs and technological advances, developed in the southern area of Mexico, specifically in Yucatan and also within the territory of Guatemala and Honduras.
But what happened in those years?
What did they do to consider themselves one of the most developed civilizations in the world?
The history of the Mayans begins like that of other great societies: they were looking for a place to settle. In the case of our protagonists, the first place chosen was the Neck, in Belize, around 2600 BC.
That era was marked by the heyday of urbanism and large-scale construction, as well as a meaningful intellectual and artistic development. There were a great variety of city-states that were involved in a network of alliances and enmities.
Finally, the Spanish "solved" the problem in their own way: colonizing and invading all of America, making all those who did not want to obey them disappear.
Now that we know who the Mayans were, let's see some of the curiosities of this civilization. It is worth mentioning that this Central American pre-Hispanic culture stood out for study and observe the galaxy, as well as in the construction and architecture of monumental buildings.
This means that, according to the Mayans, the human being can do two things: feed his hatred and eliminate the human species from the Earth, or on the contrary evolve towards harmonic association with the universe and understand that he is nothing more than a piece. more of the puzzle that forms the planet Earth, the Milky Way, and everything else that is beyond the limits of our galaxy.
It is worth mentioning that many important data on the Mayan culture were destroyed with the colonizing arrival of the Spaniards, who considered the local Mayan culture as “the work of the devil”, it only remains to imagine everything that could have been known about the Mayans if all this information was not spoiled.
