El hydrogen it is one of the most important chemical elements existing from any point of view, being precisely the most abundant in the entire galaxy thanks to a percentage of approximately 84%.
2 What is the hydrogen cycle like?
4 How is it obtained?
It is a colorless gas, toilet e tasteless and is the essential element for the formation of water, as well as organic matter. There are 3 isotopes of hydrogen: protium, which is found in more than 99,98% of the natural element; deuterium, which is found in nature in 0,02%, and tritium which appears in very small quantities in the natural environment and which can be produced artificially through nuclear reactions.
The energy that comes to us from the sun and gravity make the water go from one physical state to another, moving between the oceans, the air, the earth's crust and living beings. The main processes of the hydrogen cycle are:
Condensation: the water vapor rises and, as it does so, condenses into droplets that will constitute the clouds.
Precipitation: it is produced when the water droplets cool down and come together to form larger drops that end up precipitating due to their greater weight.
Once we have reached the last process, it restarts and the hydrogen cycle begins again.
Although we know that it is an element found in the Universe, it is rather scarce on planet Earth. So let's see how hydrogen arrives to our body.
The lack of this element would cause the imbalance in the body pH and electrolytes, which would make us sick. This item we find it in the form of water and in acidic foods, like lemons, oranges or mandarins, in short, in fruit trees known as citrus, so that we will only have to drink and consume them to be able to have hydrogen.
Plants, like other living beings, need hydrogen to be able to live and grow normally. And they, like us, They take it from the water they absorb through their roots which are found below the ground most of the time, or on the branches of other plants, such as epiphytic orchids of the genus Phalaenopsis, for example.
What is hydrogen used for?
At an industrial level it is used for fossil fuel refining like oil and in the sulfur removal, in addition to get solid fats from vegetable oils unsaturated liquids. But its most widespread use is in the ammonia production, since a single molecule of Nitrogen binds 3 of hydrogen, forming NH3.
The safest way to obtain it is from the electrolysis of water. However, other raw materials used for this purpose are hydrocarbons, natural gas, crude oil or fuel oil.
Is extremely flammable, and if mixed with air it explodes. As if that were not enough, if we inhale large concentrations of this gas we will have oxygen deficiency; and our situation will be more serious if we have a respiratory disease.
And with this we end. I hope you now know more, not only about the hydrogen cycle, but also about the element.
