What is procrastination and why does it happen?
When a person procrastinates too much ends up feeling unproductive, then feelings of sadness and even anxiety arrive. The longer something that needs to be done takes, feelings are usually not good at all, but then why do people procrastinate so often when it does them no good?
Not everyone procrastinates, there are people who have a clear vision of their life and work, and are always directed towards their goals. First they do one thing and when they finish it they go on to another, as simple as that ...
3 Causes of procrastination
5 Immediate gratification
Procrastination is doing the least urgent tasks first or doing the most pleasant things instead of the least pleasant (and probably the most important). In this way, impending tasks are delayed for later.
A procrastinator treats every responsibility as a threat to his "freedom." Therefore, they fight it!
However, the procrastinator must learn to increase the value of certain priorities, even if they do not really enjoy doing them, in order to remain productive in all aspects of their lives. The public perception (bosses, friends, family, co-workers ...) of those who procrastinate is the belief that aversion to tasks is accompanied by laziness, little willpower, irresponsibility and low ambition.
There may be a connection to anxiety issues, low self-esteem, and a self-defeating mindset. Procrastination is strongly related to a lack of self-confidence (eg, low self-efficacy or learned helplessness) or dislike of the task (eg, boredom and apathy).
It is critical to understand that these justifications serve a very important purpose: allow them to continue procrastinating by minimizing the perceived effects of actions and allow them to continue to feel good with respect to who we are as people. They try to be in control of their lives and what they have to do, although when they don't do what they should, it creates anxiety and stress.
We automatically choose the easy way, trying to experience a brief moment of joy, rather than suffer the pressure of unavoidable responsibility.
You lose twice, because there is no benefit to having half a day spent on mindless activities, and there is no chance that work done in a hurry will be good. Therefore, anger comes with ourselves for not having been able to overcome this need to procrastinate, and we are dissatisfied because at the end of the day we still have most of our work to finish.
You may need to learn to stop procrastinating.
