Within the literature in Spanish stands out even in our times as one of the greatest Spanish writers Garcilaso de la Vega, of whom there was no marked work throughout his years of life (1498-1536), his poems having been collected and published some time later in the same sixteenth century, thus making it easier for us to get to know all the greatness of his talent.
Within his work, one of the most important or at least most remembered points is his Canticle of Salicio and Nemoroso, an eclogue that in part served to refer to his forbidden love for Isabel Freire, who in the end ended up marrying another person, being a serious blow to Garcilaso. Apart from his pastoral eclogues we find epistolary essays and a Petrarchan songbook of 40 sonnets and 5 songs.
Without a doubt his best known work is Real Comments, which was published in 1609. The work was written from his own childhood and youth memories of prominent figures from the Viceroyalty of Peru.
