1 What is the periodic table and what is it for?
4 What is the importance of the table of elements?
But they are not there by chance, but their placement and the data that said table gives us, has a purpose. This purpose will be to know both the similarities and everything that differs to each of the main elements.
Group 7: manganese (Mn), technetium (Tc) and rhenium (Re), all of them belong to the Manganese family.
Group 11: Copper (Cu), silver (Ag) and gold (Au) are known as coining metals, although it is not a term that everyone accepts.
To understand this organization, you have to know that each one of the members of a group has very similar characteristics as their electronic configuration and the same valence, that is, the number of electrons they have in the last shell. Of course, when we look from top to bottom and in the same group, we will see how the atomic radii of each of the elements that make it up increase.
If we now focus on the horizontal rows that make up the periodic table, then it leads us to talk about the periods. Depending on which period each element belongs to, it will indicate the number of energy levels of an atom.
2 period: In this case, the atomic number increases a little more and we will find a total of eight elements, among which are lithium, boron, carbon or nitrogen, among others, as we see in the image.
6 period: Another 18 elements are found in the so-called sixth row, or period 6. Some of them are cesium, tungsten or mercury.
Block s: The block s corresponds to the first two groups, that is, the alkaline and the alkaline earth, without forgetting helium and hydrogen.
Block d: Groups 3 to 12 would be in this block. You can say that the transition metals are in it.
Symbols: The symbol is the element representation. It has an uppercase letter and is sometimes accompanied by other lowercase letters, depending on the case.
Color of the elements: Indicates its reflection of light when we talk about normal conditions.
