Snapshot of Recent UK Immigration News: Second Half of 2021 Publication
In this article, we look at some of the latest UK immigration related announcements from the second half of 2021.
Published in August 2021, theSponsorship Roadmapsets out changes which were implemented from 1 December 2020. These include:
suspension of the 20,700 cap on the number of skilled workers
The Home Office are also looking to:
review fees introduce an enhanced Skilled Worker eligibility checking tool
Between 2022 and 2024, there are further plans to streamline the sponsorship journey including:
the re-use of information already held by the government such as automatic checks against data held by other departments
In addition, the Home Office will launch in Spring 2022, the new“Global Business Mobility”route for overseas business wishing to establish a presence in or transfer staff to the UK. This seems to be revising/amalgamating existing work visa categories, for example the currentIntra Company Transfercategory.
The Migration Advisory Committee (or “MAC” for short) carry out consultations and determine what jobs they consider to be a shortage occupation in the UK.
As a result of Brexit, there is no longer an endless supply of EU labour and many business sectors such as care homes are struggling to fill roles and/or pay the necessary minimum salary required under the sponsorship rules.
It seems to be a good overview for employers looking to take the first step and embark on the sponsorship journey.
For quite some time, the Home Office have stood firm that EEA nationals resident in the UK by 31 December 2020, must make an application for status under the EU Settlement Scheme deadline of 30 June 2021.
On 6 August 2021, through apress release, the Home Office have said that these individuals “who apply late to the EU Settlement Scheme (EUSS) will have their rights protected” whilst their application is being determined. This will offer some security and stability for both individuals (and their family members), as well as for employers, though we always recommend checking the latest guidance first.
Towards the end of July 2021, the Department for Business, Energy & Industrial Strategy published theirUK Innovation Strategy.
This route appears to be for graduates from top global universities wishing to come to the UK to work. It seems that no job offer is required and it offers flexibility to the visa holder for changing employers.
We would expect the currentGraduatevisa category to remain open, as this is open exclusively to foreign students who are graduating or have recently graduated from a UK higher institution. Further Information
